 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. **Delivery uses self-hosted WebSockets.** Committed changes are published to socket sessions; browsers subscribe and receive updates with subsecond latency. The store provides replayable revisions for catch-up after disconnect. Connection management and reconnection logic live in the delivery component; browser sync handles subscription, reconnect, and catch-up.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Self-hosted WebSockets were chosen over five-second HTTPS polling and hosted realtime services. Polling would add repeated reads and 5-second lag; hosted services would add recurring fees and provider coupling. The project accepts the operational work of connection capacity and reconnect logic to keep fast updates without vendor dependency.
 
